3.1  RAM and the memory hierarchy

TL;DR
  - Every level trades size for speed. Rough orders of magnitude: registers
    under 1 ns, L1 1 ns, L2 4 ns, L3 15 ns, RAM 100 ns, NVMe SSD tens of µs,
    network to another continent 100 ms.
  - RAM (DRAM) keeps each bit in a tiny capacitor that leaks: it must be
    refreshed constantly, and it loses everything when powered off.
  - Bandwidth comes from channels: two modules in the right slots work in
    parallel and double it; one module alone halves it.

      sudo dmidecode -t memory | grep -E 'Size|Type:|Speed|Locator'
      lsmem                     # memory blocks the kernel sees

On your machine
  L1 data   4 × 48.0 KiB, each shared by 2 CPUs
  L1 code   4 × 32.0 KiB, each shared by 2 CPUs
  L2        4 × 1.2 MiB, each shared by 2 CPUs
  L3        12.0 MiB, shared by all 8 CPUs
  Line      64 bytes: the block memory moves in

  RAM       10.5 GiB available of 15.5 GiB (68%) · 1.1 GiB completely free
  Cache     7.5 GiB in buff/cache, 604.0 MiB of it shared (tmpfs)
  Swap      256.0 MiB used of 8.0 GiB · swappiness 60

Pitfalls
  - Latency hardly improved in 20 years; bandwidth did. Code that chases
    pointers waits on latency, code that streams arrays enjoys bandwidth.
  - The rated speed (DDR5-6000) often needs a profile (XMP or EXPO) turned on in
    the firmware; otherwise the RAM runs at a safer default.
  - DDR5's "on-die ECC" only protects inside the chip: it isn't ECC memory and
    reports nothing (3.3).
  - "Free" RAM is wasted RAM: Linux fills it with cache (17.1).

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 3.1 --why

